// The durable key-value store: state that outlives a run. `store.set` writes, `store.get` reads
// (`found` / `absent`, so a stored `null` never blurs with a missing key), `store.get_or` reads at a
// TYPE with a fallback, `store.list` enumerates a directory. Keys are AMBIENT — an operation names a
// path and a WORKSPACE decides where it lands, so `store.workspace(path = "playground/store")`
// prefixes every key inside it. And because two runs (or two parallel branches of one) can
// read-modify-write the same key and lose an edit, the SAME install also serves `store.exclusive`: a
// critical section its serial FIFO runs one at a time. One word, both halves — a prefix without its
// lane was how a read-modify-write quietly ended up in the project-wide root FIFO.
//
// These operations escalate to the RUNTIME, which machine-answers them against the project's durable
// rows (never a human), so this module runs with no handler of its own — and a second run sees what the
// first one wrote.

@"Read a counter, add one, write it back — and answer the new value. The whole body is ONE critical
section: `store.exclusive` runs it inside the nearest workspace's sequential FIFO, so two concurrent
bumps can never read the same count and both write the same successor.

The read is `store.get_or`, the TYPED store read: a durable cell is an `unknown` no schema guards (an
older run's shape, a hand edit in the console), so every read is a boundary read — T comes off the
fallback, and a missing OR unreadable cell answers the 0 this counter starts from."
agent bump_section(value: null) -> unknown with store.get | store.set {
  let current = store.get_or(key = "visits", fallback = 0)
  store.set(key = "visits", value = current + 1)
  current + 1
}

@"Bump a durable counter ATOMICALLY. The section above is handed to `store.exclusive`, whose answer
crosses the request boundary as `unknown` — a type filter narrows it back, and the `case _` arm is what
a caller writes instead of trusting a boundary it did not check."
agent bump(value: null) -> integer with store.exclusive {
  match (store.exclusive(task = bump_section)) {
    case integer(count) -> count
    case _ -> 0
  }
}

@"The root-served section: the same read-modify-write with NO workspace installed, so the
`store.exclusive` rides to the run root and the RUNTIME itself serves it as the outermost serial
domain. The task runs at the PROJECT ROOT (the key is un-prefixed) in the project-wide durable FIFO, so
concurrent runs' sections still never interleave — which is why the key spells its own directory."
agent root_bump_section(value: null) -> unknown with store.get | store.set {
  let current = store.get_or(key = "playground/root_visits", fallback = 0)
  store.set(key = "playground/root_visits", value = current + 1)
  current + 1
}

@"Entry (the ROOT-SERVED domain): bump the counter with no workspace installed at all."
agent main_root() -> string with store.exclusive {
  let visits = match (store.exclusive(task = root_bump_section)) {
    case integer(count) -> count
    case _ -> 0
  }
  f"root visits=${string.to_string(value = visits)}"
}

@"Entry: the two halves of the store's defence of a NAME. A name a model or a user proposed becomes a
path segment, so `a/b` is two directories and `..` climbs out of the workspace a facility exists to
confine — and the geometry every other agent here relies on is only as sound as the strings put into it.

`store.safe_segment` is the TOTAL half: it canonicalises a proposal (trimmed, lower-cased) or answers
`null`, which is an ordinary outcome the app renders back to whoever proposed the name.
`store.workspace` is the other half: a path that is not made of such segments PANICS, because a program
that opened a workspace on unvalidated input has a DEFECT — there is no other directory it could have
meant, and answering a value would let the run keep writing under the bad prefix. The panic is caught
here only to show it; a real program validates first and never reaches it."
agent guarded_names() -> string with store.get | store.set | store.delete | store.list {
  let accepted = match (store.safe_segment(name = "  Scribe  ")) {
    case null -> "(rejected)"
    case name -> name
  }
  let refused = match (store.safe_segment(name = "../escape")) {
    case null -> "(rejected)"
    case name -> name
  }
  let defect = {
    use handler {
      request panic(msg: string) { break f"panic caught: ${msg}" }
    }
    use store.workspace(path = "..")
    "unreachable"
  }
  f"accepted=${accepted} | refused=${refused} | ${defect}"
}

@"Entry: open a workspace, bump a durable counter twice, write and read a memo, and list the
workspace. The declared row lists all four store operations because `store.workspace` re-performs
every one outward (prefixed) to the runtime — even the ones this body does not use."
agent main() -> string with store.get | store.set | store.delete | store.list {
  // ONE install, both halves: every key below is prefixed with `playground/store/`, and
  // `store.exclusive` is served by this workspace's serial FIFO for the rest of the block.
  use store.workspace(path = "playground/store")

  // 1. Atomic counter: two bumps, each a critical section of the workspace's serial domain. The first
  // bump's new count is deliberately dropped — only the second one's is shown — and `let _ =` is how a
  // deliberate discard is spelled (an unbound one warns, K3028).
  let _ = bump(value = null)
  let visits = bump(value = null)

  // 2. Plain write / read: `store.set` stores any value; `store.get` distinguishes found from absent,
  // which is the read to reach for when a MISSING key must be told apart from a wrong-typed one.
  store.set(key = "greeting", value = "hello")
  let greeting = match (store.get(key = "greeting")) {
    case store.found(value => value) -> json.stringify(value = value)
    case store.absent(key => missing) -> f"(absent: ${missing})"
  }

  // 3. The typed read of a key nobody has written: `get_or` answers the fallback for an absent cell
  // and for one holding the wrong shape alike, so a reader never branches on either.
  let nickname = store.get_or(key = "nickname", fallback = "(unnamed)")

  // 4. List the workspace: a `leaf` per value-holding key, a `branch` per subdirectory below it.
  let names = for (let element in store.list()) {
    next match (element) {
      case store.leaf(key => key) -> key
      case store.branch(name => name) -> f"${name}/"
    }
  }

  f"visits=${string.to_string(value = visits)} | greeting=${greeting} | nickname=${nickname} | keys=${json.stringify(value = names)}"
}
